## Key `version` ## {#version-handling-key-version}

The value must be a non-empty [=string=].

<div class=issue>
Engines disagree sharply on the accepted grammar. Chromium accepts 1 to 4
dot-separated components, each a decimal integer with no leading zero in
the first component (a leading zero in a later component is tolerated
and silently canonicalized with a developer-facing warning); a version
outside this shape fails to load. Gecko accepts any string: a
"recommended" grammar (at most 4 parts, each `0` or `[1-9][0-9]{0,8}`) is
documented and a mismatch produces a warning, but the value is used
as-is regardless, and Gecko's own comparator treats non-numeric parts as
meaningful rather than invalid. WebKit imposes no grammar at all; only
emptiness is checked, and even that does not block the extension from
loading at the engine level.

This specification needs to pick one behavior, or explicitly scope
`version` to the intersection grammar that all three already accept
without a warning.
</div>

## Key `version_name` ## {#version-handling-key-version_name}

An optional [=string=] providing a version to show to users in place of
[[#version-handling-key-version|`version`]], for example to include a channel qualifier such
as "1.2 beta". It has no effect on [=extension version comparison=] and is
not required to follow the [=version string=] grammar.

Issue: Not implemented in Gecko. The `management` API schema documents an
optional `versionName` output field, but no code path reads a manifest
`version_name` key or ever populates that field, so it is currently
vestigial there. Chromium and WebKit both support it as display text
that falls back to `version` when absent or empty.

# Version number handling # {#version-number-handling}

## The `version` string ## {#version-string-grammar}

An extension's <dfn>version string</dfn> is the value of its
[[#version-handling-key-version|`version`]] manifest key.

To <dfn>parse a version string</dfn> given |input|:

1. Let |parts| be the result of [=strictly split=]ting |input| on U+002E (.).
1. If |parts|'s [=list/size=] is 0, return failure.
1. Let |components| be an empty <a data-link-type="dfn" data-link-spec="infra">list</a>.
1. For each |part| of |parts|:
    1. If |part| does not consist entirely of ASCII digits, return failure.
    1. If |part|'s [=string/length=] is greater than 1 and |part|'s first
        code point is U+0030 (0), return failure.
    1. [=list/Append=] the result of interpreting |part| as a base-ten
        integer to |components|.
1. Return |components|.

<div class="example">
`"1.2.3"` parses to «1, 2, 3». `"01.2"` and `"1..2"` both fail to parse.
</div>

Issue: This algorithm describes Chromium's actual grammar (see the Issue
under [[#version-handling-key-version]]) as the strictest of the three, so that it can serve
as a common baseline. Neither Gecko nor WebKit implement this parse step as
a rejection; both accept a [=version string=] that fails to parse under this
algorithm and store it unchanged. Whether this specification requires
engines to reject an unparseable `version`, and whether it caps the number
of components (Chromium: 4) or the value of each component (Chromium:
0-4294967295), is open.

## Comparing version strings ## {#version-comparison}

To <dfn>compare two version strings</dfn> |versionA| and |versionB|:

1. Let |a| be the result of [=parse a version string|parsing=] |versionA|.
1. Let |b| be the result of [=parse a version string|parsing=] |versionB|.
1. If |a| or |b| is failure, [=version comparison=] is not defined by this
    algorithm; behavior is engine-specific (see Issue below).
1. Let |n| be the larger of |a|'s and |b|'s [=list/size=].
1. For each |i| in the range 0 to |n|, exclusive:
    1. Let |x| be |a|[|i|] if |i| is less than |a|'s [=list/size=],
        otherwise 0.
    1. Let |y| be |b|[|i|] if |i| is less than |b|'s [=list/size=],
        otherwise 0.
    1. If |x| is greater than |y|, return "|versionA| is newer".
    1. If |x| is less than |y|, return "|versionB| is newer".
1. Return "equal".

<div class="example">
Comparing `"1.2"` and `"1.2.0"` returns "equal": the missing third
component of `"1.2"` is treated as 0.
</div>

<div class="example">
`"4294967295.0"` and `"1.0"` both [=parse a version string|parse
successfully=] under Chromium's grammar and under Gecko's (Gecko only warns
about the 10-digit first component; it does not reject it). The two engines
order them oppositely:

- Chromium parses the first component as the literal integer 4294967295
    (it fits exactly in a 32-bit unsigned value) and so treats
    `"4294967295.0"` as newer than `"1.0"`.
- Gecko's comparator parses the same component as a signed 32-bit integer;
    4294967295 overflows that type, and Gecko silently substitutes 0 on
    overflow rather than rejecting the value or saturating it. Gecko
    therefore treats `"4294967295.0"` as equal to `"0.0"`, and so *older*
    than `"1.0"`.

The two engines disagree about which of `"4294967295.0"` and `"1.0"` is
newer, for two version strings that both engines accept without error.
</div>

<div class=issue>
The algorithm above is Chromium's plain numeric tuple compare, given as a
normative starting point because it is the simplest of the three. It is
not what Gecko or WebKit do. Gecko's comparator decomposes each
dot-separated part into `<number><string><number><string>`
and compares the string segments byte-wise, treats a `+` suffix specially
(incrementing the preceding number and normalizing to "pre"), treats a
bare `*` part as infinity, and silently maps any out-of-range or
unparseable numeric segment to 0 rather than erroring. This makes
Gecko's ordering observably different from a numeric tuple compare for
the same input, as the worked example above shows, and also lets Gecko
order two version strings that are not comparable at all under the
algorithm above (for example `"1.0a1"` vs `"1.0b1"`). WebKit does not
implement version comparison anywhere in the engine at all: `version` is
stored and returned as an opaque string, and if any comparison for
update purposes happens, it happens outside the engine, in the host
application.

This specification needs to decide whether to standardize Chromium's
numeric-tuple algorithm, Gecko's richer algorithm, both (with engines
free to choose), or to leave comparison entirely implementation-defined
and unobservable to extensions.
</div>

## Invalid version strings ## {#invalid-version-strings}

<div class=issue>
The three engines disagree about what happens when `version` is missing,
empty, or does not [=parse a version string|parse=]:

* Chromium fails to load the extension entirely, with an install error.
* Gecko never fails to load the extension for a malformed `version`: any
    non-empty string is accepted and used as-is, with only a developer
    console warning when it does not match the recommended grammar.
    Gecko also accepts an empty string for `version`, unlike the other
    two engines.
* WebKit records an internal error when `version` is missing or empty,
    but does not itself refuse to construct or load the extension over
    it; whether the host application (Safari) then blocks installation is
    unknown.

This specification should state whether an extension with an invalid
`version` must be rejected at load, and if so, at what granularity (the
whole extension, or just the key).
</div>

## Observability to extensions ## {#version-observability}

Version comparison is not exposed to extension JavaScript as an API in any
of the three engines examined; it is purely an install/update-time concern
of the browser.

[`runtime.getManifest()`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#extension-runtime) returns the [=version
string=] exactly as authored, unmodified, in all three engines: it reads
directly from the parsed manifest object rather than from any canonicalized
form.

`runtime.getVersion()` does not behave identically across engines. Where it
canonicalizes its input before parsing (Chromium), calling it can return a
string different from `runtime.getManifest().version` for the same
extension (for example `"1.002.3"` in the manifest becomes `"1.2.3"` from
`getVersion()`). Where an engine performs no canonicalization (WebKit), the
two calls always agree. Gecko does not implement `runtime.getVersion()` at
all.

Issue: Should `runtime.getVersion()` be required, and if so, must it return
the literal manifest string or a canonicalized form? The two are
observably different in at least one shipping engine today.
